1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,586
Two traders are looking at the same chart.

2
00:00:08,036 --> 00:00:12,399
The first says: I feel price will go up.

3
00:00:12,849 --> 00:00:19,195
The second says: this happened 500 times before, and price went up 290 times.

4
00:00:19,646 --> 00:00:23,277
The first is a guess. The second is a count.

5
00:00:23,726 --> 00:00:27,202
This counting approach is called quant trading.

6
00:00:28,202 --> 00:00:32,956
Hello, this is episode one of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

7
00:00:33,406 --> 00:00:38,239
We start from zero. No coding and no heavy maths needed yet.

8
00:00:38,689 --> 00:00:46,395
Three questions today. What is quant trading? What is an edge? And what are the parts of a quant system?

9
00:00:47,395 --> 00:00:52,541
Quant comes from quantitative. Meaning: counting, numbers.

10
00:00:52,991 --> 00:00:56,622
Here decisions come from data, not feelings.

11
00:00:57,072 --> 00:01:02,140
The method is simple. First write a rule. Then check it on past data.

12
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:05,750
And only trade when the numbers look right.

13
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:12,079
Think of a tea seller. Every day he notes how many cups he sold.

14
00:01:12,529 --> 00:01:18,329
After a few months he sees: 100 cups on a normal day, 150 on a rainy day.

15
00:01:18,779 --> 00:01:23,297
So he made a rule: if clouds show up, buy more milk.

16
00:01:23,747 --> 00:01:27,717
He looked at data, found a pattern, and made a rule.

17
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:32,295
That is quant thinking. Markets work the same way.

18
00:01:33,295 --> 00:01:35,333
There are two kinds of trading.

19
00:01:35,783 --> 00:01:40,928
First, discretionary. You look at the chart and decide each time.

20
00:01:41,378 --> 00:01:47,178
Second, systematic. The rules are written in advance; you just follow them.

21
00:01:47,628 --> 00:01:50,868
Both can make money, and both can lose it.

22
00:01:51,318 --> 00:01:57,038
The difference: a systematic approach can be tested first. And mood stays out of it.

23
00:01:58,038 --> 00:02:01,121
What does a rule look like? A simple example.

24
00:02:01,571 --> 00:02:08,650
If price closes above the 20-day high, buy. Stop 2%, target 4%.

25
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:12,418
A rule should be clear enough for a computer to understand.

26
00:02:12,868 --> 00:02:20,025
"Buy when it feels strong" is not a rule. And the one above is only an example, not advice.

27
00:02:21,025 --> 00:02:24,186
Now the most important word: edge.

28
00:02:24,636 --> 00:02:29,965
An edge is a small advantage that shows up over many trades.

29
00:02:30,415 --> 00:02:34,620
Imagine a coin that lands heads 55 times out of 100.

30
00:02:35,071 --> 00:02:40,477
One toss tells you nothing. Over 1000 tosses the difference is clear.

31
00:02:40,928 --> 00:02:50,176
A casino does not win every hand. But over thousands of hands it stays ahead. A quant thinks like the casino, not the gambler.

32
00:02:51,176 --> 00:02:53,055
Now a small calculation.

33
00:02:53,506 --> 00:02:58,261
100 trades. 40 wins, 300 rupees each.

34
00:02:58,710 --> 00:03:01,950
60 losses, 100 rupees each.

35
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:10,367
Wins 12,000, losses 6,000. Net 6,000. That is an average of 60 rupees per trade.

36
00:03:10,817 --> 00:03:17,165
This is called expectancy. The win rate was only 40%, yet the maths is positive.

37
00:03:18,165 --> 00:03:21,796
But note this. Anything can happen in 10 trades.

38
00:03:22,245 --> 00:03:25,954
Even a good system can have 5 losses in a row.

39
00:03:26,405 --> 00:03:29,488
An edge needs many trades to show itself.

40
00:03:29,938 --> 00:03:34,692
That is why a quant does not judge one trade. They judge the whole series.

41
00:03:35,692 --> 00:03:38,852
A quant system has five parts.

42
00:03:39,303 --> 00:03:43,273
One, the idea. A belief about the market.

43
00:03:43,722 --> 00:03:50,803
Two, data. Three, the backtest: running the rule on past data.

44
00:03:51,253 --> 00:03:55,222
Four, risk. How much to put on one trade.

45
00:03:55,673 --> 00:04:01,786
Five, execution. Running the rule without interfering, by hand or with a bot.

46
00:04:02,786 --> 00:04:07,148
Now an honest point. Quant trading is not magic.

47
00:04:07,597 --> 00:04:09,793
It comes with no guarantee.

48
00:04:10,243 --> 00:04:15,075
A backtest can look great and the system can still fail live.

49
00:04:15,525 --> 00:04:20,854
And markets keep changing. An edge that exists today can shrink tomorrow.

50
00:04:21,854 --> 00:04:25,485
Everyone asks: do I need to know coding?

51
00:04:25,935 --> 00:04:29,984
Not at the start. Learn the thinking first, then the tools.

52
00:04:30,434 --> 00:04:36,782
In this series we start with a notebook and Excel, and slowly move to code and bots.

53
00:04:37,782 --> 00:04:39,349
So, today's summary.

54
00:04:39,799 --> 00:04:45,207
Quant trading means decisions from data and rules, not feelings.

55
00:04:45,657 --> 00:04:52,266
An edge is a small advantage that shows over many trades. Its number is expectancy.

56
00:04:52,716 --> 00:05:01,388
Five parts of a system: idea, data, backtest, risk, execution. And no guarantee anywhere.

57
00:05:02,388 --> 00:05:09,624
Next episode: the truth about win rate. Why can a system that wins 90% of the time still blow an account?

58
